 - Outputs/reference files should be JSON arrays. Each element may be a string or object containing `text`/`output` fields.
 - Future work: support tabular CSV, scored datasets, multi-turn conversations.
 
+## Thresholds
+- CLI option `--threshold <metric>=<min>` (repeatable) allows gating CI on metric scores.
+- Example: `frai eval --outputs results.json --threshold exact_match=0.9 --threshold toxicity=0.95`.
+- Exit code 0 if all thresholds pass, 1 if any fail, 2 if thresholds are malformed or reference unknown metrics.
+- JSON reports gain top-level `passed` boolean and `thresholds` array with `{ metric, min, score, passed }` entries.
+- Markdown reports gain a "Thresholds" section showing PASS/FAIL status.
+- Metrics with null scores (e.g., exact_match without references) fail their thresholds.
+- SDK users can pass `thresholds` object to `generateReport({ ..., thresholds: { exact_match: 0.9 } })`.
+
 ## Extensibility Hooks
 - `frai-core/src/eval/metrics.js` exposes `DEFAULT_METRICS`; additional metrics can be registered by passing custom evaluators to `runEvaluations`.
 - `writeReport` accepts `format` argument to enable additional exporters (HTML, CSV) later.
 - CLI parsing accommodates further evaluation subcommands (e.g., `frai eval bias`).
+- Thresholds work with custom metrics passed to `runEvaluations` or registered as `DEFAULT_METRICS`.
 
 ## Follow-up Tasks
 1. Integrate real toxicity/bias models once provider abstraction is implemented (Phase 0 → Providers milestone).
